WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.896
[SQUEAKING][RUSTLING][CLICKING]

00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:10.760
JONATHAN GRUBER:
Today, we're going

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.150
to talk about externalities.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.350
And we'll start with
some simple theory--

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.630
so the theory of externalities.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:23.550
Now, as I've said before,
the fundamental mantra

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.340
of most this course is
that markets know best.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.770
Indeed, as I said, the
first fundamental theorem

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.180
of welfare economics--

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.390
that powerful-sounding
name-- is the conclusion

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.570
that the perfectly competitive
private market will deliver

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.820
the welfare-maximizing outcome.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.730
So standard economic starts
from the proposition the market

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.330
knows best.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:47.720
In that, world the government
can only screw things up, OK?

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.430
All the government does is
sort of mess up the first best.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.170
Now, either, despite
this, the government

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.900
is, in the typical
developed economy, at least,

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.760
typically, 25% to 30% of the
economy is the government.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.980
So either these
developed economies

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.250
have it totally wrong,
or there are some reasons

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.010
why we might actually depart
from the standard 14.01

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.080
model of thinking for
reasons for government.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.170
Now, we didn't cover one, which
was imperfect competition.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.600
We talked about it in
perfectly competitive markets,

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.768
government policy can
make things better.

00:01:20.768 --> 00:01:22.310
What I'll do in the
next two lectures

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.360
is talk about two
other kinds of ration--

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.142
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:01:25.142 --> 00:01:27.600
So for [INAUDIBLE],, we talked
about imperfect competition,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.683
and obviously, redistribution
is another rationale

00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:30.308
for government.

00:01:30.308 --> 00:01:32.100
We talked about
that last time, too.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.160
If we think that
social welfare is

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.030
increased by redistributing
from rich to poor,

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:39.990
despite some leakage--

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.310
[AUDIO OUT] So we've
covered two roles.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.170
There's fixing
imperfect markets,

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.560
and there's redistribution.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.977
In the next two lectures, we'll
talk about two other roles

00:01:48.977 --> 00:01:49.610
for government.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.068
And if this whets your appetite
and you want to learn more,

00:01:52.068 --> 00:01:55.250
you could take 14.41, which is a
course all about public policy.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.565
You can learn a lot more.

00:01:56.565 --> 00:01:58.190
But we'll talk about
two other reasons.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.830
Today, we're going to
talk about externalities,

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.268
which is another justification
for government involvement.

00:02:04.268 --> 00:02:06.310
And we're going to dive
right in with an example.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.419
So what's an externality?

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:13.990
An externality occurs whenever
the actions of one party

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:18.760
impact another party in a way
for which the first party does

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.710
not receive the
costs and benefits.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:25.210
So if my actions
impact you, but I

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.220
don't bear the cost if
they're negative actions

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.410
or their benefits if
they're positive actions,

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.700
then that's an externality.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.010
So any time one party's
actions affect another party,

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:36.840
but the first part
does not receive

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.540
either the costs or the
benefits of that impact, that's

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.680
an externality.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.093
Let's start with the
standard theory example, OK?

00:02:44.093 --> 00:02:45.760
The standard example
we always teach now

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.880
is the example of
the steel plant

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.470
dumping sludge in a river.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.690
So imagine somewhere
in America, there's

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.920
a steel plant located on a
river, and they produce steel.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.490
And the production process has
a certain production function.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.650
And one feature of this
production function

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.560
is for every unit of steel
produced, a unit of sludge,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.520
a unit of waste, is produced.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.100
And the steel plant dumps
that into the river.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.530
So just given the nature of
their production process,

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.460
one unit of steel equals
one unit of sludge

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.800
is dumped in the river.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.280
Now, let's imagine
that down this river,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.350
there's a set of fisherman.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:25.400
And the fishermen make their
living fishing in the river,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.090
and that when the steel plant
dumps sludge in the river,

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.070
it flows downriver
and kills their fish,

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:34.860
kills some of their fish, OK?

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.960
Now, this is a
classic exempt of what

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:44.670
we call a negative
production externality.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.740
It's production
in the sense it's

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.210
coming from the supply side.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:51.450
It's coming from the
production of the steel.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.020
It's producing this externality.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.050
It's negative because
it's negatively impacting

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.000
the fishermen down the stream.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.340
And it's an externality
because the steel plant

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.182
doesn't give a shit.

00:04:03.182 --> 00:04:04.140
They dump their sludge.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.630
It goes down, and
it kills some fish.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.970
And they care, but they don't
bear the cost of doing so.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:09.637
The fishermen bear
the cost of doing so.

00:04:09.637 --> 00:04:13.100
It's an externality, because
the plant dumps the sludge,

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.930
but it's the fishermen
who bear the consequences.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.260
It's a classic
negative production

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.420
externality-- "negative"
because it's bad,

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.550
"production" because it
comes from the supply side,

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.220
"externality" because the costs
are borne by another party,

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.950
not by the party
doing the activity.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:31.010
Graphically, we can analyze that
in something like figure 23-1.

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:34.100
This is a classic
externality diagram.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.140
Here we have the
market for steel.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.060
We look at the market
for steel, because it's

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.480
the production of
steel that generates

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:40.620
this negative externality.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.620
We look at the market for steel.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:44.970
You've got a quantity
of steel being produced,

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.340
and a price of steel--

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.270
standard market.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:49.790
You've got a supply
curve and a demand curve.

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:51.450
So in our analysis to date--

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.600
and let's be
perfectly competitive.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.160
Our analysis to
date, we say where

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:59.000
supply equals demand
is at point A,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.550
with a quality Q1
and a price P1.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.570
And we would say that is
the welfare maximizing

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:08.090
outcome, perfectly
competitive market, OK?

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:12.560
What this misses, however,
is that the supply curve

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.817
f is the private marginal cost.

00:05:15.817 --> 00:05:17.900
That's the cost of the
machines, and the building,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.660
and the workers
that make the steel.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.170
But we as a society don't just
care about the private cost.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.720
We care about the social cost.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.610
We care about the fact that--

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.010
think of a society with the
social welfare function.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.343
That social welfare function
incorporates both the steel

00:05:33.343 --> 00:05:34.532
plant and the fishermen.

00:05:34.532 --> 00:05:36.740
So we don't just care about
the steel plant's costs--

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.270
the machines and the workers.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.250
We care about the costs
and the fishermen, too.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.310
We care about social
marginal costs.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.080
What are social marginal costs?

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.010
That is private marginal
costs plus the damage done

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:51.525
to the negative externality.

00:05:51.525 --> 00:05:53.150
And what we're going
to do is we should

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.240
assume that's $100 per
unit of steel produced.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.170
We're just making that up.

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:59.630
We're just going to assume
that every steel unit produced

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.280
delivers $100 in negative
externality in terms of fish

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:03.690
killed downstream.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.892
Now, that does not have--

00:06:05.892 --> 00:06:07.100
I haven't seen this constant.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.270
It doesn't have to be constant.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:09.920
You can imagine a little
sludge doesn't do much damage,

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:11.990
but if you dump more
sludge, it does more damage.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:15.140
That would be a non-linear
curve that wouldn't [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:18.440
But to make life
easy, imagined sort

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.620
in this region,
every unit of steel

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:27.560
produces a unit of sludge that
kills $100 worth of fish, OK?

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.050
And meanwhile-- now, on the
other hand, the demand curve--

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.790
so the supply curve is
the private marginal cost.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.590
The demand curve is the
private marginal benefit.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.605
But in this case, that is the
same as the social marginal

00:06:40.605 --> 00:06:41.980
because, there's
no externalities

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:43.635
from the consumption of steel.

00:06:43.635 --> 00:06:46.010
We'll come back in a minute
to consumption externalities.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.592
But let's assume for a minute
that my private benefit

00:06:48.592 --> 00:06:50.300
from consuming steel
is society's benefit

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.175
from consuming steel.

00:06:51.175 --> 00:06:52.670
It's not like by
consuming steel,

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:56.210
I create some positive or
negative externality, OK?

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.220
So the demand curve is
both a private and social

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:01.640
marginal benefit, but
the private marginal cost

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:03.673
is below the social
marginal cost--

00:07:03.673 --> 00:07:05.840
the social marginal cost,
the private marginal cost,

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.720
plus the externality damage.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.560
Now, what does this mean?

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.530
This means that
the optimal level

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:16.280
of production from society's
perspective is not point a.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.520
It's point c.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.790
From society's perspective,
incorporating the damage

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:25.550
to the fishermen along with
the cost of the steel plant,

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:29.510
then the optimum point is
where the social marginal cost

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:33.250
equals social marginal
benefit, which is 0.2.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.060
So from the society's
perspective,

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.930
there should be less
steel production, OK?

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.630
Add an amount to 2.2,
which is below 2.1.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.150
But the steel
plant doesn't care.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.500
The steel plant produces T1.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:47.830
They don't care about
some fishermen downstream.

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:51.660
So you have an overproduction
and a deadweight loss.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.660
Now, here's the key thing
about deadweight loss.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:54.910
We've talked about
deadweight loss before,

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:56.827
but here's the new trick
with deadweight loss.

00:07:56.827 --> 00:07:59.210
Deadweight loss is always
drawn with reference

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:04.180
to social marginal benefit
and cost curves, not private.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.800
So deadweight loss
is relative to what's

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.800
best for society, not
what's best for one

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.800
particular actor in society.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:16.230
So the deadweight loss is all
the units between Q2 and Q1

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:19.280
where the social
marginal cost exceeds

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:21.970
the social marginal benefit.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:25.310
So for that first unit
to the right of Q2,

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.570
there's this tiny
deadweight loss.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.680
But as you move further
and further from Q2 to Q1,

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.210
the deadweight loss grows.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.752
Because those are
units where there's

00:08:33.752 --> 00:08:37.309
a bigger and bigger gap between
the social marginal cost

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:39.650
and the social margin benefit.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:44.059
The bottom line is, we're
back to chain of logic.

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:47.220
The private steel plant does
what's privately optimal.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.050
That's with private
marginal cost

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:51.240
equal to private
marginal benefit.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.880
That's at point a.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.720
From a social perspective, we
care about social marginal cost

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.403
and social marginal benefit.

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:59.320
Social marginal benefit
is the same as private

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.310
marginal benefit
in this example.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:04.070
But social marginal
cost includes the cost

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:06.550
to the fisherman,
so it's higher.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.310
Given that it's higher,
we'd socially optimally

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:12.790
produce less steel, OK?

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:17.820
And therefore, we would want
less production of steel.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:20.560
Now, once again, there's a
lot of complications here.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.100
For example, what if we,
instead of using less steel,

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:24.917
we just stop them
from polluting?

00:09:24.917 --> 00:09:26.250
Well, that would be another end.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:27.300
There's lots of
complication here,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:28.950
but for now, just assume
that's the way the production

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:30.867
function works, If you're
going to make steel,

00:09:30.867 --> 00:09:32.550
you're going to pollute, OK?

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:35.070
So that's the outcome.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:35.970
That's what we call--

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:37.928
that's how we think about
a negative production

00:09:37.928 --> 00:09:39.900
externality.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.242
Now, externalities can also
happen on the consumption side.

00:09:44.242 --> 00:09:46.200
And the classic example
of negative consumption

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:54.440
externality is smoking.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:58.010
So let's go to figure 23.2.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:00.493
Now we have the
market for cigarettes.

00:10:00.493 --> 00:10:01.910
There's the quantity
of cigarettes

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:05.390
on the x-axis, the price of
cigarettes on the y-axis.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:08.808
Now, here, we're going to assume
the private marginal costs

00:10:08.808 --> 00:10:10.850
curve is equal to the
social marginal cost curve.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:11.990
That is, there's no--

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.550
maybe, when they make tobacco
for cigarettes, they pollute.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.395
But we'll ignore
that for now, OK?

00:10:16.395 --> 00:10:18.020
We're just going to
assume that there's

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.390
no pollution from
the production side.

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:24.260
But on the consumption side,
when I smoke a cigarette,

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:28.220
I do damage to other people, OK?

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:29.910
Most perniciously,
most obviously,

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:31.010
through secondhand smoke.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:32.940
I'm very sensitive
to this right now,

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:36.110
because my wife's mom
smoked three packs a day.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:39.830
My wife now has COPD, Coronary
Obstructive Pulmonary Disease,

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:41.990
and was just in the
hospital with pneumonia,

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.820
because her mom smoked
three packs a day,

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:45.600
and so her lungs are weak.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.600
So this is the example
of a negative consumption

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:48.860
externality.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:54.070
Her mom smoked, and smoked
to the point of point a,

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:57.500
where her private benefit
is equal to private cost

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:01.100
but the social cost includes
my wife, I like to think.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:06.230
And therefore the benefits
include the negative impacts

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:07.650
on my wife.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:10.690
So this led my wife's
mom to over-smoke.

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.440
Or in general, people
over-smoke because they

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.530
don't account for the fact
that there's damage on others.

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:20.720
Therefore, there is
too much smoking.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.700
Too much smoking, because
it doesn't account

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.470
for the damage on others.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.780
But in this case, this is
not a change in supply curve.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:29.240
It's a reduction
in the benefits.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.430
Because the production of
cigarettes hasn't changed.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.350
It's just the value of consuming
cigarettes has changed.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:36.268
Cigarettes are less
valuable to consume

00:11:36.268 --> 00:11:38.810
because you get enjoyment from
smoking-- at least some people

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:40.160
think it's enjoyable.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.410
But offsetting that is the
damage you doing to others.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:47.710
So that the shift downward of
the marginal benefit curve.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:50.410
Now, the social
marginal benefit is

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.700
below the private
marginal benefit.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:57.030
And the new equilibrium is where
the social marginal benefit

00:11:57.030 --> 00:12:01.960
equals the social
marginal cost, OK?

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:04.510
And therefore, the
optimal level of smoking

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:07.954
is lower than what individuals
would privately choose.

00:12:07.954 --> 00:12:08.454
Yeah?

00:12:16.238 --> 00:12:18.280
STUDENT: Where did you
get the margin at the end?

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:20.150
Because there is still profit
margin in the business.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:22.400
JONATHAN GRUBER: Yeah, so
that's a very good question.

00:12:24.577 --> 00:12:26.910
I'm going to come back to
what happens when you depart--

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:28.630
we're assuming perfectly
rational people

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:29.710
with perfect information.

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:30.920
I'll come back to this.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.080
This gets more
complicated [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:35.038
This is a perfectly
rational person [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:35.038 --> 00:12:36.280
even before they know it.

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:37.430
I'm not saying it's--

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:39.410
I'm not saying
they're bad people.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.948
I'm not saying smokers are
bad people, because they're

00:12:41.948 --> 00:12:43.240
doing what's privately optimal.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.632
There's nothing bad about
doing what's privately optimal.

00:12:45.632 --> 00:12:47.600
But it doesn't matter
if they knew or not.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:49.300
The point is,
they're doing damage.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:52.052
From a social perspective,
there's too much smoke.

00:12:52.052 --> 00:12:52.552
Yeah?

00:12:56.595 --> 00:12:57.600
Yeah.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:12:59.742
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:59.742 --> 00:13:00.700
JONATHAN GRUBER: Right.

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:04.502
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
example [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:04.502 --> 00:13:06.335
JONATHAN GRUBER: Where
does $0.40 come from?

00:13:06.335 --> 00:13:06.720
STUDENT: $0.40.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.053
JONATHAN GRUBER: OK, so $0.40
cents per pack is actually

00:13:09.053 --> 00:13:11.130
an empirical estimate
of the external cost,

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.210
the external damage
done by smoking.

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.312
So it's actually
quite interesting, OK?

00:13:15.312 --> 00:13:17.520
So basically-- now, this is
without secondhand smoke.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.560
Think about the
exercise in smoking.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.080
So for example, you get
sick, and your medical costs

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:23.393
are higher.

00:13:23.393 --> 00:13:25.560
Actually, if you get external
costs, because smokers

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:26.970
start a lot of fires.

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:28.845
Thousands of people die
every year in America

00:13:28.845 --> 00:13:30.347
in fires started by smokers.

00:13:30.347 --> 00:13:32.430
That's an external cost,
because other people die,

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:34.770
and the fire department--
things like that.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:37.070
Smokers are less productive,
so firms make less money,

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:39.570
because they have to take smoke
breaks and things like that.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:44.250
You add all that up, and
you get $0.40 a pack.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.335
Now, that's without
secondhand smoke.

00:13:46.335 --> 00:13:47.710
The problem with
secondhand smoke

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:49.910
is, we don't know how
big that affect is.

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:52.410
The estimates are someone
between $0.01 a pack and $2.00

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.040
a pack.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.760
So it's just really,
really hard to measure.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:59.400
And it's really hard to measure
partly because of a really

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.020
interesting phenomena.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:03.240
Which is, you might have
come back to me and said,

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:04.650
wait a second.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:08.070
What happened to your wife
is not an externality.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.580
Why would-- under what
situation-- here's

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.150
a hard question.

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:15.540
Under what set of assumptions
would the damage done

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:20.663
to my wife by her mom's
smoking not be an externality?

00:14:20.663 --> 00:14:22.080
Let's say her mom
was still alive.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:25.704
You could see her
in the hospital.

00:14:25.704 --> 00:14:26.204
Yeah.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:31.352
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:31.352 --> 00:14:33.560
JONATHAN GRUBER: Exactly,
if only [INAUDIBLE] her mom

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:34.643
is going to bear the cost.

00:14:34.643 --> 00:14:37.210
But if her mom knew
that by smoking--

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.130
this is where information
comes in-- knew

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:42.400
that by smoking, she'd
make her daughter sick,

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.690
and chose to smoke anyway,
then it's not an externality.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:49.840
In that case, in the
marginal benefits for the mom

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:53.560
would be included the
damage done to the daughter.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.690
So what's really
interesting, externalities

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:58.940
is not a strictly
easy thing to measure.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:01.590
It depends on-- now, the
fisherman [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:02.752
floats down the river.

00:15:02.752 --> 00:15:03.460
That's different.

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:06.460
But here, the
externality is only true

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:08.020
if the mom didn't account.

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:09.670
So the key thing,
the key question

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.550
that you have to ask
is not, is do families

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:15.683
maximize a family
utility function

00:15:15.683 --> 00:15:17.100
or an individual
utility function?

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:19.060
If her mom was maximizing
her individual [INAUDIBLE]----

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:20.477
sorry to rag on
my mother-in-law--

00:15:20.477 --> 00:15:23.090
may she rest in peace--
but it's a good example.

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:26.020
If she was measuring-- if
she was just focusing on her

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.510
individual utility--

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.295
then she should just
consider the benefits

00:15:30.295 --> 00:15:34.030
to her of smoking versus the
cost of a pack of cigarettes.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:36.770
But if she was maximizing
family utility,

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:38.680
she should take the
benefits to her smoking

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:41.830
minus the damage it does to
her daughter versus the cost

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:42.910
of a pack of cigarettes.

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:44.555
Yeah?

00:15:44.555 --> 00:15:50.722
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:50.722 --> 00:15:53.180
JONATHAN GRUBER: Ah, well, no,
But that's-- you're assuming

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:54.250
the family utility function.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:56.170
That's what I mean by
family utility function.

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:58.150
I mean you care about
people in your family.

00:15:58.150 --> 00:16:02.390
Now, basically, the interesting
question is, in other words,

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:03.410
it's just about weight.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:05.120
Let's say at one extreme,
a purchase is a function,

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.070
I don't care about
anyone in my family.

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:09.040
A full family utility
function, I treat them

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.480
as if they are myself.

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:12.040
I care about the [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:13.900
Where do people line in between?

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:16.520
Now, in fact, there's
some evidence on this.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:18.400
And the evidence is
basically that women

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.520
care about their
families more than men.

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.335
So there's actually
a number of studies

00:16:23.335 --> 00:16:24.870
in developing countries.

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:26.840
There's a cool way to test this.

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:29.950
And the way you test
this is you say,

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:32.960
if family utility
maximization holds,

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:34.630
then it shouldn't
matter who controls

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:36.310
the resources in the family.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.913
If I take $1 from a wife
and give it to a husband,

00:16:38.913 --> 00:16:41.080
that shouldn't change
anything, because they're both

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:43.417
maximizing the same
family utility function.

00:16:43.417 --> 00:16:45.250
But if there's individual
utility functions,

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:47.320
then who controls the
resources will determine

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:48.456
how the money gets spent.

00:16:48.456 --> 00:16:51.900
And there was a quite
striking example from the UK

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.013
where they had a tax
break for children.

00:16:54.013 --> 00:16:55.930
And it used to be what
happened-- this is back

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:57.820
in the '70s-- it use
to be what happened

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:00.130
was, for every child
in the household,

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:03.640
at the end of the year, a
check got sent to the house.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:06.760
Well, typically, women were
home and controlled the money

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:07.520
in the house.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:08.929
So the women got that check.

00:17:08.929 --> 00:17:11.460
They then changed the law
and said, no, instead, we're

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:14.419
just going to let take-home
pay be a bit higher.

00:17:14.419 --> 00:17:15.836
So instead of
sending a check, let

00:17:15.836 --> 00:17:17.140
me see your check each
week, we'll include it

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:18.293
in your take-home pay.

00:17:18.293 --> 00:17:19.960
Well, the take-home
pay went to the men.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:21.680
Men were working, women weren't.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:23.680
So it's shifted from women
controlling the money

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:25.329
to men controlling the money.

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:27.060
And what they found
was this shift

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:29.710
led to much less being spent on
children's clothes and books,

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:32.202
and much more being spent
on alcohol and tobacco,

00:17:32.202 --> 00:17:34.410
which is inconsistent with
a family utility function.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.618
It's consistent with men
caring less about their kids

00:17:36.618 --> 00:17:38.040
than women, OK?

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.820
So the bottom line is, we don't
have a perfect family utility

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:41.540
function.

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:43.440
Probably we don't have a perfect
individual utility function

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:43.720
either.

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:45.500
Probably, people care
somewhat about their family.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:47.170
But as long as it's not a
perfectly family utility

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:49.600
function, then there's some
externality on family members

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:50.650
from secondhand smoke.

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:52.050
So $0.40 is clearly too low.

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:54.850
We just don't know how low, OK?

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:58.270
Now, externalities don't
have to be negative.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:00.640
They can also be positive, OK?

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.990
Let's think about a positive
consumption externality.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:07.930
And let's come to one
of my favorite people

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:11.110
I like talking shade of,
or throwing shade to,

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.240
which is my neighbor, OK?

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:17.170
I don't get along with
my neighbor at all,

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:21.880
and basically, part of the
reason I don't get along

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:24.940
with my neighbor is because
my neighbor has a tendency

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.160
to start massive
landscaping projects,

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:29.640
and then leave them half
done, and just not do

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:30.880
anything for 10 years.

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:32.530
And it's unattractive.

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:36.790
And so basically, let's
imagine that my neighbor

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:38.590
is looking outside his yard.

00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:39.500
And he's thinking
about the landscape.

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:41.167
And he's thinking,
what's it worth to me

00:18:41.167 --> 00:18:43.620
to fix this dirt
pile in my yard--

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:46.210
which is, now, finally,
after 20 years, fading away.

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:47.840
But it was there
for about 20 years.

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:50.170
What's it worth to me to fix
this dirt pile in my yard?

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:54.670
OK, well imagine that
the dirt pile will

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:59.580
cost $1,000 to haul away, but he
only gets $800 of this utility

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:01.310
from looking at it.

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:05.052
So let's say I get $500 of
utility from not looking at it.

00:19:05.052 --> 00:19:06.760
And let's say-- this
is totally true-- he

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:08.710
doesn't care about me.

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:12.340
Then he'll say privately, I
shouldn't move the dirt pile.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:14.890
My benefit from
doing so is $800.

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:18.700
My cost of doing
so is $1,000, OK?

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.620
So the cost is a [INAUDIBLE]
if I don't do it.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:22.950
But socially, he should
get rid of the dirt pile,

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:25.060
because the benefit to
society is the $800 to him

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:27.118
and the $500 to me.

00:19:27.118 --> 00:19:28.660
So if he removed
the dirt pile, there

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:30.805
would be a positive externality.

00:19:30.805 --> 00:19:33.257
For him to consuming
landscaping services,

00:19:33.257 --> 00:19:34.840
there would be a
positive externality,

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:37.620
which is my improved view.

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:40.780
So the positive consumption
externality, OK?

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:43.510
So that is-- you could think
of this thing as figure 23.2,

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:44.890
but just flip it now.

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:49.390
Think of my consumption having
a marginal social benefit.

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:52.100
In that case, unlike
the case of cigarettes,

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:54.678
where there's too
much consumption,

00:19:54.678 --> 00:19:56.095
now there's too
little consumption

00:19:56.095 --> 00:19:58.260
of landscaping services
by my neighbor,

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:01.300
Because he's not accounting
for the benefit to me, OK?

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:01.800
Yeah?

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:07.950
Let me come to that
in one minute, OK?

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:11.260
Now, finally, there's
one last example,

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:13.680
which is you could have
positive production externality.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:15.730
And the classic example
of positive production

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:19.500
externality is research and
development by companies.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:22.657
When companies do
R&amp;D, it benefits them,

00:20:22.657 --> 00:20:24.240
but it also produces
general knowledge

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:27.850
that benefits everyone,
including their competitors.

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:32.250
As a result, if you go
back to figure 22, 23.1,

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:35.820
my social marginal costs
of doing R&amp;D are actually

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:38.310
lower than my private
marginal costs,

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:42.587
because I'm producing
benefits for other produces.

00:20:42.587 --> 00:20:44.670
It's the opposite of dumping
sludge in the stream.

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.280
I'm dumping knowledge
in the stream, OK?

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:50.040
And that knowledge benefits
everybody downstream.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:53.490
Indeed, the estimates are that
$1.00 of R&amp;D is worth about

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:56.190
twice as much to society as it
is to the individual firm to do

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:56.690
it.

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:01.260
Indeed, if you'd like to
learn more about this,

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:03.980
you can pre-order my new
book, Jump-Starting America,

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:06.200
which talks about how we're
going to make America run

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:08.330
better again by
investing in public R&amp;D.

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:09.390
So I talk all about it.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:10.906
Very excited about
that right now.

00:21:13.163 --> 00:21:14.830
So those are the kinds
of externalities,

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.150
positive and negative.

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:21.730
Now, the question
many ask is, so what?

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:23.110
So these things exist.

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:24.650
Why can't people
just figure it out?

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:26.980
Take me and my neighbor, OK?

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:28.960
There's an easy solution here.

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:33.510
I just go to my neighbor,
and I pay him 200 bucks--

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:35.450
or $201.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:37.400
He's $1 better off.

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:39.820
I'm $299 better off.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:41.110
Problem solved.

00:21:41.110 --> 00:21:45.878
Indeed, there's a famous
economist named Ronald Coase--

00:21:45.878 --> 00:21:49.305
Coase-- who proposed that
there is no such thing, really,

00:21:49.305 --> 00:21:52.340
as an externality,
because all externalities

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:58.630
can be internalized through
private negotiation.

00:21:58.630 --> 00:22:02.470
So as long-- here's the key--

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:05.592
as long as there's
well-defined property rights.

00:22:05.592 --> 00:22:08.050
So in the case my neighbor,
there are well-defined property

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:08.830
rights.

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:09.775
He owns the dirt pile.

00:22:09.775 --> 00:22:11.650
So I go to him and offer
him money to fix it,

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:12.840
to get rid of it.

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:15.853
Likewise, imagine the
fisherman owns the river.

00:22:15.853 --> 00:22:17.770
They could then go to
the steel plant and say,

00:22:17.770 --> 00:22:21.580
we are going to charge you for
dumping sludge into the river.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:24.220
And we'll charge you enough
so that your private costs go

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:27.790
to the social costs, OK?

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:30.740
So basically, Coase said
there's no problem here.

00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:32.480
There's no need for government.

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:34.600
The private market
can take care of it.

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:37.000
Well, that's a really
cute argument in theory,

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.575
but in practice, it breaks down.

00:22:38.575 --> 00:22:40.450
Because think about that
argument for the big

00:22:40.450 --> 00:22:42.075
biggest [INAUDIBLE]
in the world today,

00:22:42.075 --> 00:22:44.200
which is global warming, OK?

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.800
Global warming happens because
I drive my car and people

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:48.477
in Bangladesh drown.

00:22:48.477 --> 00:22:50.560
How are they supposed to
come to me and say, John,

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:51.580
you should drive less.

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:53.747
I'm going to pay you to
drive less so I don't drown.

00:22:53.747 --> 00:22:55.300
And that's just crazy, right?

00:22:55.300 --> 00:22:56.990
It doesn't work for
big externalities.

00:22:56.990 --> 00:22:58.990
It doesn't always work
for little externalities.

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:00.580
So think about yourself, OK?

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:04.240
Think about your neighbor
playing music too loud.

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:06.125
You have a 14.01
test the next day.

00:23:06.125 --> 00:23:07.500
Your neighbor's
done with finals.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.490
They're partying, playing
their music too loud.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:12.760
That's a classic negative
externality on you.

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:14.530
Now, in principle,
you should say, well,

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:15.670
I can figure out how
much it's worth to me

00:23:15.670 --> 00:23:17.563
and go pay them to
turn their music down.

00:23:17.563 --> 00:23:19.730
But in practice, you're not
going to do that, right?

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:21.670
You'd be, like,
a social outcast.

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:22.210
OK?

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:23.740
In fact, people
don't work this way.

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:26.720
There's a famous story about an
economist-- it can't be true,

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:28.060
but it's a wonderful story--

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:30.055
who was on a plane
trying to get work done.

00:23:30.055 --> 00:23:31.930
The person next to him
wouldn't stop talking,

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:35.170
so he offered them
5 bucks to shut up.

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:38.290
Now, that would be
the Coasean solution.

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:40.285
But in reality, life
doesn't work that way.

00:23:40.285 --> 00:23:41.660
So in fact, the
private market is

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:43.760
unlikely to solve these
kinds of problems.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:44.260
Yeah?

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:54.320
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] what
does it cost [INAUDIBLE]??

00:23:56.893 --> 00:23:58.560
JONATHAN GRUBER:
That's another problem.

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:00.950
There's lots of reasons why
you can't-- there's no way.

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:02.947
Even Coase bless
his soul, would not

00:24:02.947 --> 00:24:05.280
take the private negotiation
to solve the global warming

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:05.780
problem.

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:08.360
There's no way, OK?

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:13.540
So that means we need
the big bad government.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:16.690
We need government solutions,
because the private market

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:18.220
has failed.

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:21.760
The private market has failed
because the private equilibrium

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:24.400
is not welfare-maximizing, OK?

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:27.215
So if you go back to
23.1, the private market

00:24:27.215 --> 00:24:31.330
has failed because the private
market overproduces steel.

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:33.808
Therefore, there is a role
for the government to,

00:24:33.808 --> 00:24:35.350
potentially, fix
this market failure.

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:37.553
It doesn't mean the
government will, OK?

00:24:37.553 --> 00:24:38.470
This is the key thing.

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:40.360
I emphasize this in my course.

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:43.090
The way to think about the
logic of government involvement

00:24:43.090 --> 00:24:45.200
in the economy is
with two steps.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:48.250
One is, is there
a market failure?

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:51.245
That is, will the private
market fail to maximize welfare?

00:24:51.245 --> 00:24:52.870
And two is, can the
government actually

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:55.670
make it better as opposed
to making it worse?

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:57.010
So just because if I have a
market failure, it doesn't mean

00:24:57.010 --> 00:24:57.920
there's a role for
the government.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.287
It means there's a potential
role for the government.

00:25:00.287 --> 00:25:01.870
Well, let's talk
about, theoretically,

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:03.662
what the government
might do, and, in fact,

00:25:03.662 --> 00:25:06.250
what the government
actually does do.

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:09.650
So theoretically, the
government has two options.

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:11.720
The first option is regulation.

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:17.740
So going to figure 23.1,
the government could summon

00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:21.580
and say, hey, steel plant,
we've done the math,

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:23.980
and you're optimal level
of steel production is 2.2,

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:26.800
so we're going to
regulate you so you

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:29.140
can't produce more than 2.2.

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:30.880
And if the government
did that, we

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:32.440
would get to the
optimal outcome.

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:36.660
If the government said,
I'm setting a max at Q2,

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:39.310
then we get the optimal
outcome, which is 2.2 will be

00:25:39.310 --> 00:25:41.740
[INAUDIBLE].

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:44.100
The problem with that
is exactly the problem

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:46.407
we talk about when we talk
about regulating monopolies,

00:25:46.407 --> 00:25:48.490
which is that requires a
huge amount of government

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:49.802
information.

00:25:49.802 --> 00:25:52.010
They need to know the shape
of the supply-- they need

00:25:52.010 --> 00:25:54.010
to know the shape
of the supply curve,

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:55.870
the shape of the demand
curve, and the size

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:56.662
of the externality.

00:25:56.662 --> 00:25:58.930
They need to know
all three things--

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:01.450
supply, and the size
of the externality--

00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:03.940
in order to get that right.

00:26:03.940 --> 00:26:06.352
And if they get it
wrong, they could

00:26:06.352 --> 00:26:07.810
lead to-- if they
get it too wrong,

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:09.227
they could shut
down a steel plant

00:26:09.227 --> 00:26:11.390
that's still totally
valuable to have running.

00:26:11.390 --> 00:26:13.060
So we have this
conversation at length

00:26:13.060 --> 00:26:16.600
with monopoly regulation--
all the same problems of life.

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:19.330
That's why economists
typically prefer

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:22.930
a simpler solution,
which is taxation,

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:28.600
or, what we call in economics
"corrective taxation."

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:32.230
And this is exciting, because
when I taught taxes last time

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:34.930
and the time before, taxes
caused deadweight loss.

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:36.940
Taxes caused inefficiencies.

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:39.730
But actually, in the
case of externalities,

00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:42.520
taxes can reduce
deadweight loss.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:43.270
How's that?

00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:46.060
Let's go to figure 23.3.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:48.230
Back to the steel market.

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:51.350
Imagine I don't know
supply and demand curves.

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.500
I just know the size
of the externality.

00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:55.820
It's $100 a unit of steel.

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:57.340
So I simply go to
the steel plant

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:00.130
and say, look, for every
unit of steel you produce,

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:01.540
I'm going to charge you $100.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:02.650
You do what you want.

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:04.400
I don't know where
you're going to end up,

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.850
but I'll charge you $100.

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:09.160
That means that
the steel plant--

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:11.830
the steel plant's private
marginal cost curve

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:15.520
will now align with the
social marginal cost curve.

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:18.370
You've essentially done
what Coase wanted us to do.

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:20.143
You've internalized
the externality,

00:27:20.143 --> 00:27:22.060
but done through government
action rather than

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:24.070
private negotiation.

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:27.280
You said, you are
imposing a social cost

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:28.840
above and beyond
your private cost.

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:30.460
We are going to
essentially make you

00:27:30.460 --> 00:27:32.850
consider-- it's like moving
to a family utility function.

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:34.030
We're going to make
you consider all

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:35.347
of society in your decision.

00:27:35.347 --> 00:27:37.180
We'll make you internalize
the damage you're

00:27:37.180 --> 00:27:38.000
imposing in your decision.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:39.583
And the way we're
going to internalize

00:27:39.583 --> 00:27:41.970
that is by charging
you for the extra costs

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:45.640
you're imposing on society, OK?

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:50.730
So that is what we call
"corrective taxation," OK?

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:55.490
And corrective taxation
leads to the optimal outcome.

00:27:55.490 --> 00:27:59.060
If we say to your steel plant,
for every unit you produce,

00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:02.360
we are now going to tax
you by amount MD, which

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:06.590
is the marginal damage, then it
leads them to actually choose

00:28:06.590 --> 00:28:07.490
point B--

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:10.330
which is confusing, because it
was point C on the other graph,

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:11.060
OK?

00:28:11.060 --> 00:28:13.010
To choice point D--

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.700
OK, it was D on
the other graph--

00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:16.260
which is the optimal outcome.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:19.460
You could say that the
system is that [INAUDIBLE]----

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:21.270
which is the optimal outcome.

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:26.810
It will cause them to choose
the optimal outcome, OK?

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:29.960
So we've essentially
got the private market

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:33.040
to do the right thing by
forcing them to internalize

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:34.732
the externality,
much as Coase would

00:28:34.732 --> 00:28:36.970
hope it would happen
through private negotiation,

00:28:36.970 --> 00:28:40.850
we've caused to happen
through corrective taxation.

00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:43.760
Likewise, we can think of
the same thing with smoking.

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:50.060
So go back to figure 23.2
Imagine I levied a tax of $0.40

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:52.430
a pack on cigarettes.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:56.010
Then the marginal benefit of
smoking would fall by $0.40.

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.010
My willingness to pay--
remember, we [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:58.010 --> 00:28:59.820
analyze taxes [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:01.570
When we say you have
a tax, it essentially

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:04.130
shifts down the demand
curve, tax on consumers.

00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:06.140
It lowers my willingness to pay.

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:08.120
I'm only now willing to
pay $0.40 less a pack,

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:10.460
because I have to pay
$0.40 in tax every pack.

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:12.193
That shifts my
demand curve down.

00:29:12.193 --> 00:29:13.610
Plus, the private
marginal benefit

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:15.260
now equates to social
marginal benefit.

00:29:15.260 --> 00:29:18.890
Boom-- no more
externalities of smoking.

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:20.210
So here's the crazy thing.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:23.240
Taxes have now removed
the deadweight loss.

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:24.990
Taxes haven't created
the deadweight loss.

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.210
Taxes have removed
the deadweight loss.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:30.400
So in a perfectly competitive
market with no market failure,

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:32.660
taxes create deadweight loss.

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:35.450
But once there is market
value, taxes can actually

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:38.360
offset deadweight loss, OK?

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:45.050
And that's the theoretical case
for government intervention.

00:29:45.050 --> 00:29:47.630
Theoretically, the government
can make things better

00:29:47.630 --> 00:29:50.640
through opposing
corrected taxation.

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:51.140
OK?

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:53.291
Questions about that?

00:29:53.291 --> 00:29:54.632
Yeah?

00:29:54.632 --> 00:29:56.870
STUDENT: Elasticity
plays into it.

00:29:56.870 --> 00:29:59.950
If somebody is
really inelastic, has

00:29:59.950 --> 00:30:04.637
really inelastic consumption
of cigarettes, would this tax--

00:30:04.637 --> 00:30:06.470
JONATHAN GRUBER: You
just need a bigger tax.

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:06.980
STUDENT: OK.

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:07.430
JONATHAN GRUBER: Yeah.

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:09.560
So basically, the way
elasticity plays into it-- now,

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:10.850
if they're totally
inelastic, you'd

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:13.100
have to wear something else,
Nothing's ever perfectly.

00:30:13.100 --> 00:30:18.230
But essentially, you could take
the diagram, and essentially--

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:19.120
no, I'm sorry.

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:20.300
No, let me back up.

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:22.670
I said that incorrectly.

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:25.490
Elasticity doesn't matter for
the actual government policy.

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:30.690
All I want to do is impose the
size of the externality, OK?

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:35.990
If, demand is inelastic, that
could mean people love smoking.

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:39.630
And as a result, you won't
reduce smoking that much.

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:41.840
But that's OK,
because basically,

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:45.320
all we care about is
correcting the externality, OK?

00:30:45.320 --> 00:30:47.680
We're not trying
to punish smokers.

00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:49.640
We're not trying to
achieve an optimal--

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:52.770
and that's the difference
between regulation

00:30:52.770 --> 00:30:54.035
and taxation.

00:30:54.035 --> 00:30:56.660
Regulation says, no, I know what
the right level of smoking is.

00:30:56.660 --> 00:30:57.620
I'm going to impose this.

00:30:57.620 --> 00:30:59.990
Taxation is, look, I don't know
the right level of smoking is.

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:01.440
Many people frickin'
love smoking.

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:02.857
Imagine if there
was externalities

00:31:02.857 --> 00:31:05.270
to consumption of insulin, OK?

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:08.860
Who wants to say, no,
less insulin, because--

00:31:08.860 --> 00:31:11.252
you want to say, no, we have
to have a ton less insulin.

00:31:11.252 --> 00:31:13.710
We should say, no, we should
tax for the externalities they

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:14.210
impose.

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:16.220
But if they still choose
to do a lot of insulin,

00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:18.860
then they're still doing the
socially optimal decision.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:20.943
It's a really good question,
because it highlights

00:31:20.943 --> 00:31:23.750
the key nature
here, is this is why

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:26.510
this framework, traditionally,
was very controversial

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:28.610
with environmental advocates.

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:30.670
Environmental advocates
hated this framework,

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:31.610
but they're like--

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:33.650
they called it a
"pollution absolution."

00:31:33.650 --> 00:31:34.750
Guess what absolution is.

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:37.700
It's this thing that
these corrupt monks

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:40.340
used to give before Martin
Luther came along to reform

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:41.697
the church back in the 1400s.

00:31:41.697 --> 00:31:44.030
Monks would come along and
say, yeah, I know you sinned,

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:47.180
but if you give me
one coin, or whatever,

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:49.105
I'll say your sin's absolved.

00:31:49.105 --> 00:31:51.730
So the monks would sort of bribe
people to get into heaven, OK?

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:52.950
It's called absoltuion.

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:55.280
So for many, this was
called pollution absolution.

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:56.510
Environmentalists hate this
way of thinking about it.

00:31:56.510 --> 00:31:57.927
They're like, wait,
you're letting

00:31:57.927 --> 00:32:00.740
people pay to get out of
the damage they're doing?

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:03.303
And the answer is, yeah,
it's the right thing to do.

00:32:03.303 --> 00:32:04.970
Because in fact, if
people pay and don't

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:06.800
change their behavior,
that means they just

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:08.150
love doing that activity.

00:32:08.150 --> 00:32:10.233
And why should you penalize
them just because they

00:32:10.233 --> 00:32:11.350
love doing that activity?

00:32:11.350 --> 00:32:14.150
We should penalize them to the
extent they're hurting others,

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:16.790
not care about the
enjoyment they get from it.

00:32:16.790 --> 00:32:19.600
So if people, for some
reason, just love smoking,

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:20.423
let them smoke.

00:32:20.423 --> 00:32:22.256
But make them account
for the damage they're

00:32:22.256 --> 00:32:25.743
doing to other people, OK?

00:32:25.743 --> 00:32:26.660
It's a great question.

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:28.512
Other questions?

00:32:28.512 --> 00:32:29.012
Yeah?

00:32:37.478 --> 00:32:40.367
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] if a
person does [INAUDIBLE]----

00:32:40.367 --> 00:32:41.700
JONATHAN GRUBER: Great question.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:43.310
I'm going to come back to it
at the end of the lecture.

00:32:43.310 --> 00:32:43.990
Great question.

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:47.180
OK, let me talk-- that's a great
segue to what do we talk now,

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:49.580
which is, let's move
from theory to practice.

00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:52.060
Let's talk about actual
government policy,

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:54.520
and what governments do,
and how it's informed

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:55.740
by this kind of framework.

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:00.510
Let's start with
environmental externalities--

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:01.360
the classic piece.

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:06.210
The classic case of
negative externalities

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:09.560
is the pollution in
the environment, OK?

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:11.870
G environmental
science, for example,

00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:14.060
we produce electricity.

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:18.290
The emissions from that
electricity, the sulfur,

00:33:18.290 --> 00:33:19.370
and the nitrous oxide--

00:33:19.370 --> 00:33:22.030
sulfur dioxide, nitro us
oxide-- go into the air.

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:24.110
They form particulate
when get into our lungs

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:25.660
and causes us to get sick.

00:33:25.660 --> 00:33:28.070
They cause acid rain,
which falls and erodes

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:29.630
[INAUDIBLE] cities,
and buildings,

00:33:29.630 --> 00:33:31.400
and things like that.

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:33.870
And then, of course,
we have global warming,

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:36.050
which is the single
biggest issue facing--

00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:40.080
maybe not you, almost certainly
your kids, and definitely

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:41.137
their grandkids, OK?

00:33:41.137 --> 00:33:42.720
Depends how long
you're going to live.

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:44.428
You guys aren't going
to make it to 2100.

00:33:44.428 --> 00:33:46.670
Yeah, probably not you.

00:33:46.670 --> 00:33:49.820
Your kids when they're old,
and your grandkids for sure--

00:33:49.820 --> 00:33:53.220
which is, basically, the
amount of fossil fuels

00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:55.160
we burn if it's loaded.

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:57.310
We now-- and the amount
of carbon dioxide

00:33:57.310 --> 00:33:58.810
in the atmosphere
is now the highest

00:33:58.810 --> 00:34:01.070
in at least 200,000 years.

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:05.030
And by estimates, including
from the Trump administration,

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:07.150
this will lead to massive
warming of the earth.

00:34:07.150 --> 00:34:09.860
And by the year 2100,
global temperatures

00:34:09.860 --> 00:34:12.750
could rise as much as
10 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:14.810
Now, for those of you
from North Dakota,

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:16.063
that might not sound so bad.

00:34:16.063 --> 00:34:18.230
Even right now in Boston,
it might not sound so bad.

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:20.000
Remember the great
line from The Simpsons

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:21.790
when it snows and
Homer goes, Hey, Lisa,

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:24.770
said there's 10 feet
of global warming.

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:29.300
But basically, the truth is it's
very bad, because basically,

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:32.540
it leads to enormous rises
in sea level by as much,

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:34.650
essentially, as 3 feet.

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:38.280
And just think about that.

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:40.940
That means, for example,
Bangladesh is gone.

00:34:40.940 --> 00:34:43.440
Bangladesh has 100
million people,

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:45.230
150 million people-- gone.

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:46.100
Cape Cod's gone.

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:47.989
New York City, largely gone.

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:49.719
Florida, gone.

00:34:49.719 --> 00:34:53.239
Basically, America
becomes the Midwest.

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:58.610
Basically, essentially,
you've got--

00:34:58.610 --> 00:35:01.870
and to put it in economic terms,
the Trump administration's

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:07.070
calculations suggest the US
will be 10% poorer by 2100

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:10.908
as a result of global warming.

00:35:10.908 --> 00:35:11.450
This is real.

00:35:11.450 --> 00:35:13.110
The conflicts are real.

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:15.520
But it's a classic
externality, because it's

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:16.880
the ultimate externality.

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:21.290
Because every single time you
drive, you contribute to it.

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:23.710
And every single time
someone in Australia

00:35:23.710 --> 00:35:25.010
drives, they contribute to it.

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:26.510
Because it all just
goes in the air,

00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:29.030
and mixes together, and create
one level of global warming.

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:31.970
It's a completely
global externality.

00:35:31.970 --> 00:35:35.840
Any pollution of emissions
anywhere has the same effect.

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:39.603
And the effect is
very distant in space.

00:35:39.603 --> 00:35:42.020
So if you're in Minnesota, the
effect is not nearly as bad

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:43.180
as if you're in Bangladesh.

00:35:43.180 --> 00:35:45.680
It's the city is time, because
the effect is going to be way

00:35:45.680 --> 00:35:47.450
in the future, not on you.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:50.060
It's a classic Externality,
because the effects clearly

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:52.380
don't affect you.

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:54.728
And they arise from
your consumption.

00:35:54.728 --> 00:35:55.228
Yeah?

00:36:00.442 --> 00:36:02.717
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:02.717 --> 00:36:03.800
JONATHAN GRUBER: Oh, yeah.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:06.140
Well, it's not-- the
time part is what

00:36:06.140 --> 00:36:07.970
makes it a bad externality.

00:36:07.970 --> 00:36:12.380
As long as when
I drive, I do not

00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:14.553
pay any extra cost for
the marginal damage

00:36:14.553 --> 00:36:16.845
I'm going to the environment,
then It's an externality.

00:36:16.845 --> 00:36:19.480
It doesn't matter if it's going
to affect now or in the future.

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:22.580
OK, the bottom line
is, when you drive,

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:24.630
you are likely making
the environment worse.

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:29.730
Ah, OK, you live in
that environment.

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:32.640
But the problem is you,
particularly here in Boston,

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:34.140
aren't going to
feel, necessarily,

00:36:34.140 --> 00:36:36.503
the consequences of global
warming, at least initially.

00:36:36.503 --> 00:36:37.170
So you're right.

00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:40.175
If you lived in your own little
world, that's a great one.

00:36:40.175 --> 00:36:41.800
Remember, externalities
is you bear it.

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:42.990
If you'll be in your
own little world

00:36:42.990 --> 00:36:44.907
where you drove, and
made your own little rain

00:36:44.907 --> 00:36:48.120
clouds like Charlie Brown, and
you covered all the conflicts,

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:49.740
then it wouldn't
be an externality.

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:51.930
But the truth is,
that's not true.

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:54.720
Many, many people
suffer the consequences.

00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:55.680
Now, what do-- yeah.

00:36:59.138 --> 00:37:04.850
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:06.100
JONATHAN GRUBER: That's right.

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:07.640
The key issues is discount.

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:10.070
There's a ton of really
deep and hard issues here.

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.230
How do you think about
discount when the damage

00:37:12.230 --> 00:37:13.322
is 100 years from now?

00:37:13.322 --> 00:37:15.530
So let's say you took a
standard interest rate today.

00:37:15.530 --> 00:37:16.900
Today, interest rates are low--

00:37:16.900 --> 00:37:18.020
3%.

00:37:18.020 --> 00:37:19.520
Well, it's still
true that 100 years

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:23.390
from now, that means damages are
worth 20% of what they're worth

00:37:23.390 --> 00:37:24.578
today.

00:37:24.578 --> 00:37:26.870
At a higher discount rate,
they're worth effectively 0.

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:29.720
We're basically saying, if you
have any form of discounting

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:30.680
of any reasonable
magnitude, you're

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:32.222
basically saying
your great-grandkids

00:37:32.222 --> 00:37:34.460
don't matter today.

00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:36.940
Well, that seems implausible.

00:37:36.940 --> 00:37:40.425
So there's a lot of deep work on
multi-generational discounting.

00:37:40.425 --> 00:37:42.550
How do we think about
discounting for hazards which

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:43.848
are way, way off in the future?

00:37:43.848 --> 00:37:46.140
Now, it turns out, even with
reasonable discount rates,

00:37:46.140 --> 00:37:48.015
the cost is still huge
today, because they're

00:37:48.015 --> 00:37:49.298
so enormous in future.

00:37:49.298 --> 00:37:51.590
Even if we discount them
back, they're still big today.

00:37:51.590 --> 00:37:53.215
But that's the kind
of difficult issues

00:37:53.215 --> 00:37:55.592
that is really exciting
and interesting stuff.

00:37:55.592 --> 00:37:56.300
So what do we do?

00:37:56.300 --> 00:38:02.090
Well, in Europe, they've
gone the taxation route.

00:38:02.090 --> 00:38:05.110
A gallon of gas in France is $7.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:07.380
Macron has just proposed
increasing the tax,

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:09.150
and it's causing Paris to burn--

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:12.360
ironically, increasing
global warming.

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:15.387
But typically, in
Europe, gasoline

00:38:15.387 --> 00:38:17.470
is many, many times more
expensive than in the US.

00:38:17.470 --> 00:38:22.037
And basically, they have carbon
taxes that try to control this.

00:38:22.037 --> 00:38:24.120
But it proved very difficult
to do that in the US.

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.660
Indeed, when Bill Clinton
proposed a $0.03 tax

00:38:26.660 --> 00:38:28.405
on gasoline when
he first elected,

00:38:28.405 --> 00:38:30.030
it basically cost
him the the Congress.

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:31.530
Think about that, $0.03.

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:34.000
That's a daily swing
in the price of gas.

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:37.160
Gas taxes are very hard
to impose in the US.

00:38:37.160 --> 00:38:40.140
So that's why there's been
a move away from a taxation

00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:42.012
approach towards a
regulation approach.

00:38:42.012 --> 00:38:43.470
Even though
regulations [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:45.300
have other weaknesses,
there's been

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:49.590
a move toward trying to
cap global emissions,

00:38:49.590 --> 00:38:52.570
trying to regulate a
cap on global emissions.

00:38:52.570 --> 00:38:55.980
And this began with the
first such agreement

00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:59.550
negotiated in Kyoto in 1990--

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:01.290
in December '97.

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:02.070
I was at that.

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:04.385
That was pretty cool.

00:39:04.385 --> 00:39:06.510
I was part of the Clinton
administration delegation

00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:07.830
that went over there.

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:11.880
And it was crazy, because
basically, you had all these--

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:14.170
think about how hard this
is to really negotiate.

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:16.565
So let's say you're India.

00:39:16.565 --> 00:39:18.603
And you come, and you
say, wait a second.

00:39:18.603 --> 00:39:19.270
Let me be clear.

00:39:19.270 --> 00:39:22.190
In 1997, of all the
stuff in the air,

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:25.260
the US was responsible
for about 30% of it,

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:27.793
and the rest the world
combined with 70%.

00:39:27.793 --> 00:39:29.460
Then you come in and
say, wait a second.

00:39:29.460 --> 00:39:32.970
We haven't done anything
to the environment, OK?

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:33.922
We're ready to grow.

00:39:33.922 --> 00:39:35.130
We want our air conditioning.

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:36.000
We want our cars.

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:37.890
We want our-- and you say,
you say, no, wait a second,

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:40.307
you can't have that because
we screwed up the environment.

00:39:40.307 --> 00:39:41.760
Screw you!

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:42.990
OK, you guys, screwed it up?

00:39:42.990 --> 00:39:44.550
You guys fix it.

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:48.230
It's a pretty hard
negotiation you have to do.

00:39:48.230 --> 00:39:50.470
Plus, the costs are quite large.

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:52.017
So it was actually pretty cool.

00:39:52.017 --> 00:39:53.100
I was there for four days.

00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:54.300
I slept four hours.

00:39:54.300 --> 00:39:57.170
In Japan, they have this
cool coffee in a can

00:39:57.170 --> 00:39:59.610
that you just sort of live on.

00:39:59.610 --> 00:40:01.512
And we negotiated
an agreement which

00:40:01.512 --> 00:40:02.970
was not going to
solve the problem,

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:05.970
but it was going to slow
the growth of emissions.

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:08.840
But America was never actually
going to ratify the agreement,

00:40:08.840 --> 00:40:11.010
so it never went anywhere.

00:40:11.010 --> 00:40:18.390
Fast forward to 20 years later
to three years ago in Paris.

00:40:18.390 --> 00:40:20.220
We signed another
climate agreement

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:23.250
called the Paris
Climate Accord, which

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:25.440
was similar to Kyoto
in that it set targets

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:26.860
that countries could meet.

00:40:26.860 --> 00:40:28.610
And we were successful
that more countries

00:40:28.610 --> 00:40:29.990
participated in Kyoto.

00:40:29.990 --> 00:40:32.242
Countries like India and
China didn't participate.

00:40:32.242 --> 00:40:33.950
But now, most countries
that participated

00:40:33.950 --> 00:40:38.160
in the Paris Agreement limiting
emissions are-- the US.

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:39.660
In that case,
signed it, but now,

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:41.452
the Trump administration
has backed off it.

00:40:41.452 --> 00:40:43.520
So we're were not enforcing
the Paris Agreement.

00:40:43.520 --> 00:40:47.460
Now, you can completely
understand politically

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:50.970
why a leader would not want to
enforce an agreement like this.

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:54.390
You're imposing pain today
for the benefit the people

00:40:54.390 --> 00:40:56.455
you'll never even meet, OK?

00:40:56.455 --> 00:40:58.830
First of all, they live in
other countries, by and large.

00:40:58.830 --> 00:41:01.110
Second of all, they're going
to be 100 years from now.

00:41:01.110 --> 00:41:04.230
So politically, it is
unbelievably challenging.

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:07.590
But the problem is, by the
time those people are drowning,

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:10.110
it's too late.

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:14.550
Basically, even if today,
if we just suddenly stopped

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:18.355
using any fossil fuel
today-- which is impossible--

00:41:18.355 --> 00:41:21.390
but let's say that we stop
using any fossil fuels today,

00:41:21.390 --> 00:41:25.290
temperatures would still rise
for something like 300 years

00:41:25.290 --> 00:41:28.140
before they start
to fall again, OK?

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:31.380
So basically, this is an
incredibly hard problem

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:33.952
because the costs
are super distant--

00:41:33.952 --> 00:41:35.910
the benefits are of fixing
it are super distant

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:37.830
if the costs were
imposed today, And that's

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:39.030
a very, very hard problem.

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:41.610
That's why this is probably the
single biggest social problem

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:45.300
facing the world over
the next 100 years,

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:47.820
is how do we deal with--

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:49.810
the economics of
the answer is easy.

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:50.630
I mean, it tells you what to do.

00:41:50.630 --> 00:41:51.130
It's easy.

00:41:51.130 --> 00:41:52.420
You post taxes or regulations.

00:41:52.420 --> 00:41:54.320
You essentially
phase out of being

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:56.720
a fossil fuel-based economy.

00:41:56.720 --> 00:41:58.990
OK so theoretically,
it's no problem.

00:41:58.990 --> 00:42:01.998
The problem is all
political, which is basically

00:42:01.998 --> 00:42:03.790
how you get people who
are going to to bear

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:06.507
the cost in the near
term without [INAUDIBLE]??

00:42:06.507 --> 00:42:08.090
And that's it's an
enormous challenge.

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:08.590
Yeah.

00:42:11.730 --> 00:42:14.830
That's a deep question
about the nature--

00:42:14.830 --> 00:42:16.920
essentially, there's a
different equilibrium

00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:19.253
where they have more faith
in the government in New York

00:42:19.253 --> 00:42:20.370
City than in the US.

00:42:20.370 --> 00:42:21.480
Maybe that's because
you're settled

00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:23.330
by Frontier, Venture,
people who never never like

00:42:23.330 --> 00:42:24.122
to be told what to.

00:42:24.122 --> 00:42:26.350
Do I didn't quite
know what that is.

00:42:26.350 --> 00:42:28.240
But in any case, this
is different attitudes

00:42:28.240 --> 00:42:30.790
towards the
government in general.

00:42:30.790 --> 00:42:33.998
And I think, essentially, if
you're going to deal with this,

00:42:33.998 --> 00:42:35.790
you're going to have
to essentially trust--

00:42:35.790 --> 00:42:37.340
think of a social
welfare function,

00:42:37.340 --> 00:42:39.090
which is not everybody
today, but everyone

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:42.120
everyone in the whole future
with some form of discount.

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:45.000
You've got to trust that
social welfare function, which

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:46.590
is the governments.

00:42:46.590 --> 00:42:49.200
In Europe, they trust
that more than in the US.

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:52.810
But look, I don't want to
minimize how hard this is, OK?

00:42:52.810 --> 00:42:56.250
There's enormous disagreement
about how fast we should go.

00:42:56.250 --> 00:42:58.883
In fact, I was originally--

00:42:58.883 --> 00:43:00.300
one of the reasons
I went to Kyoto

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:01.970
was because the economists
in the Clinton administration

00:43:01.970 --> 00:43:04.690
were opposed to how strict
the Kyoto targets were.

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:06.000
We thought it was too severe.

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:09.390
And I actually, in
1997, was the subject,

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:11.878
I believe, of one of the
very first spam attacks.

00:43:11.878 --> 00:43:13.920
I got spammed by hundreds--
which, at that point,

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:16.830
was enormous-- of environmental
activists saying how terrible I

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:20.680
was because I didn't want to
restrict emissions more, OK?

00:43:20.680 --> 00:43:24.450
Basically, there's huge
disagreement in this.

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:26.040
But it's a topic
we can't ignore.

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.050
It's a critical topic.

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:31.360
Likewise, another critical
source of externalities.

00:43:31.360 --> 00:43:34.670
Is health externalities.

00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:37.470
Now, we've talked about
smoking, but there's

00:43:37.470 --> 00:43:38.670
lots of externalities.

00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:43.060
So smoking, the externalities
are actually not that large.

00:43:43.060 --> 00:43:45.215
$0.40 a pack is not that large.

00:43:45.215 --> 00:43:46.840
Depends on if you
use secondhand smoke.

00:43:46.840 --> 00:43:49.230
Compare that to drinking.

00:43:49.230 --> 00:43:51.240
The externalities for
drinking are enormous,

00:43:51.240 --> 00:43:54.730
because drunk drivers
kill people, OK?

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:59.390
Indeed, there are
13,000 deaths per year.

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:01.740
To be politically
incorrect, let me say it--

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:07.010
that is our 9/11s every year
from people drunk driving.

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:10.440
400,000 people are injured
every year from drunk driving.

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:12.245
That's a classic externality.

00:44:12.245 --> 00:44:14.120
If I kill someone in my
car, maybe my license

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:15.670
will get taken away.

00:44:15.670 --> 00:44:19.430
But m I'm not bearing the
full cost of killing, OK?

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:20.636
Yeah.

00:44:20.636 --> 00:44:22.570
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:22.570 --> 00:44:24.310
JONATHAN GRUBER: That's
a great question.

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:26.740
This is-- I'm trying to compress
my whole course into one

00:44:26.740 --> 00:44:27.880
lecture, which is hard.

00:44:27.880 --> 00:44:30.310
But there are estimates
of value of a life.

00:44:30.310 --> 00:44:33.220
Typically, we say a life
is worth about $10 million.

00:44:33.220 --> 00:44:35.470
We can talk another time
about where that comes from.

00:44:35.470 --> 00:44:38.230
But basically, if you
take those valuations,

00:44:38.230 --> 00:44:43.030
the externalities from
drunk driving are enormous.

00:44:43.030 --> 00:44:46.930
And yet, we have very, very low
taxes on alcohol [INAUDIBLE]..

00:44:46.930 --> 00:44:47.430
Yeah?

00:44:51.090 --> 00:44:52.663
This is a great question.

00:44:52.663 --> 00:44:53.830
We could go all day on this.

00:44:53.830 --> 00:44:55.500
Absolutely, they are.

00:44:55.500 --> 00:44:58.087
And this is why nobody
likes economists.

00:44:58.087 --> 00:45:00.420
For example, we think babies'
lives aren't worth as much

00:45:00.420 --> 00:45:01.900
as your lives.

00:45:01.900 --> 00:45:02.400
Why?

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:04.560
Because you've got more
embedded human capital.

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:06.008
You've formed more connections.

00:45:06.008 --> 00:45:07.550
The value of your
life is worth more.

00:45:07.550 --> 00:45:10.078
Tell that to the parent
of a newborn, OK?

00:45:10.078 --> 00:45:12.120
So there's all sorts of
interesting and difficult

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:13.620
issues in valuing life.

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:15.510
That's a whole separate field.

00:45:15.510 --> 00:45:21.940
But in any case, the bottom
line is, since life's valuable,

00:45:21.940 --> 00:45:24.510
there is an important
externality.

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:29.070
Now, let's come to the
interesting case, a very hot

00:45:29.070 --> 00:45:31.710
topic, which is illegal drugs.

00:45:31.710 --> 00:45:34.020
Now, let's talk about
cigarettes for a second.

00:45:34.020 --> 00:45:36.960
Cigarettes impose
externalities, but I never

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:39.180
said the answer with to
ban cigarettes or make

00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:40.630
them illegal.

00:45:40.630 --> 00:45:42.380
I said the answer
was to tax them

00:45:42.380 --> 00:45:44.358
to the extent of
their externalities.

00:45:44.358 --> 00:45:46.650
So let's say we could properly
measure secondhand smoke

00:45:46.650 --> 00:45:47.440
or whatever.

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:47.940
We do it.

00:45:49.890 --> 00:45:52.950
So indeed, cigarette taxes
today probably exceed

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:54.645
any reasonable expert
of externality.

00:45:54.645 --> 00:45:56.520
I'm one of the biggest
anti-smoking advocates

00:45:56.520 --> 00:45:59.400
out there, and I would
admit cigarette taxes today,

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:01.920
which average nationally
several dollars a pack,

00:46:01.920 --> 00:46:05.990
probably exceed the
externalities of smoking.

00:46:05.990 --> 00:46:08.480
Moreover, then, illegal
drugs, we don't just tax them.

00:46:08.480 --> 00:46:09.920
We ban them.

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:12.240
Until recently, in all
states, we banned marijuana.

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:15.900
We still banned other
drugs everywhere.

00:46:15.900 --> 00:46:16.890
Why?

00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:17.670
Think about it.

00:46:17.670 --> 00:46:20.970
The framework I taught you
so far does not say you

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:22.560
should ban anything.

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:25.070
If I love doing
cocaine, you should

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:26.740
tax the externalities I do.

00:46:26.740 --> 00:46:28.980
Indeed, let's go further.

00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:32.880
There aren't many externalities
to most illegal drugs, OK?

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:36.510
Guys who do cocaine, they
may get in car accidents--

00:46:36.510 --> 00:46:40.170
or heroin-- but they largely
just kill themselves.

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:42.323
They don't do a lot
of social damage.

00:46:42.323 --> 00:46:43.740
They largely just
kill themselves.

00:46:43.740 --> 00:46:45.157
Indeed, what are
the externalities

00:46:45.157 --> 00:46:46.320
from illegal drugs?

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:48.120
They come from
fighting illegal drugs.

00:46:48.120 --> 00:46:52.050
It's the crime that comes since
the drugs are illegal, OK?

00:46:52.050 --> 00:46:53.817
That should be
externalized from drugs.

00:46:53.817 --> 00:46:55.650
So for that reason,
economists, for decades,

00:46:55.650 --> 00:46:58.580
have argued drugs should
be legal-- all drugs.

00:46:58.580 --> 00:47:01.230
Because if you think about it,
they should be legal and taxed.

00:47:01.230 --> 00:47:03.510
Because the externalities
largely come to the fact

00:47:03.510 --> 00:47:04.690
that they're illegal.

00:47:04.690 --> 00:47:06.985
You think about all
the deaths in Mexico.

00:47:06.985 --> 00:47:08.610
Think about what
would happen in Mexico

00:47:08.610 --> 00:47:10.680
if drugs were legal in America.

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:12.218
Suddenly, the price
would collapse,

00:47:12.218 --> 00:47:14.760
the cartels would have no money
to make, and they'd break up,

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:16.802
and there wouldn't be all
these deaths in Mexico.

00:47:16.802 --> 00:47:19.416
Those deaths in
Mexico are our on us.

00:47:19.416 --> 00:47:21.608
It's on the US
consumers who demand

00:47:21.608 --> 00:47:23.400
these illegal goods
from Mexico that create

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:25.390
the demand for crime in Mexico.

00:47:25.390 --> 00:47:27.488
It's probably-- if
drugs were legal,

00:47:27.488 --> 00:47:28.530
it wouldn't be a problem.

00:47:28.530 --> 00:47:30.000
So why not legalize drugs?

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:31.970
Well, we only have one minute.

00:47:31.970 --> 00:47:34.638
So the answer is because we
don't think this model applies

00:47:34.638 --> 00:47:35.430
in all [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:35.430 --> 00:47:37.290
In particular, we don't
think people always

00:47:37.290 --> 00:47:39.080
make rational decisions.

00:47:39.080 --> 00:47:42.750
And this framework computes
rational decisions.

00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:45.060
We think that people
make mistakes.

00:47:45.060 --> 00:47:47.160
And if the mistakes
are severe enough,

00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:49.050
then taxation is not
the right answer.

00:47:49.050 --> 00:47:51.750
You need an outright ban.

00:47:51.750 --> 00:47:53.050
And that's why we do.

00:47:53.050 --> 00:47:55.320
That's why we say, look,
we think that people,

00:47:55.320 --> 00:47:59.310
most people try illegal drugs
before the time they're 18.

00:47:59.310 --> 00:48:01.437
We think that people
before they're 18

00:48:01.437 --> 00:48:03.270
don't have fully formed
prefrontal cortexes.

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:04.920
They're not fully rational.

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:07.848
They get addicted, and that
has lifelong consequences.

00:48:07.848 --> 00:48:09.390
Therefore, we make
the drugs illegal,

00:48:09.390 --> 00:48:11.598
because we think that the
costs of those consequences

00:48:11.598 --> 00:48:14.647
are so large that we don't
want to take a chance.

00:48:14.647 --> 00:48:16.980
But that raises the whole
debate we should all be having

00:48:16.980 --> 00:48:18.540
about marijuana legalization.

00:48:18.540 --> 00:48:20.310
Is that true of marijuana?

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:23.448
The externalities of marijuana
are really small, OK?

00:48:23.448 --> 00:48:24.990
Indeed, the damage
you do to yourself

00:48:24.990 --> 00:48:27.178
is actually pretty small--
much less than smoking.

00:48:27.178 --> 00:48:29.220
So how do we think about
whether marijuana should

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:31.707
be legal or illegal,
versus cocaine and heroin,

00:48:31.707 --> 00:48:33.040
and how much it should be taxed?

00:48:33.040 --> 00:48:35.100
So that's the kind of set of
interesting issues you raise.

00:48:35.100 --> 00:48:36.170
We talk about those.

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:39.330
So anyway, if you find that
more interesting, take 14.41.

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:42.230
We'll spend many lectures
talking about it.

